// Fixture: domain_multiple_domains_side_by_side
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §4.2 -- each identifier block is an independent type
// (domain); nothing in the spec suggests a `define` is limited to referencing a single
// domain. Positive-path depth fixture: two genuinely different identifier domains,
// used as two separate fields on the same `define`, both assigned correctly,
// confirming they coexist without interference (e.g. a buggy implementation that
// tracks "the current identifier type in scope" as a single global rather than
// per-field could plausibly let one domain's members leak into the other field's
// validation).

identifier ActionAttack
	melee_weapon  = "Standard attack done with a melee weapon"
	ranged_weapon = "Standard attack done with a ranged weapon"

identifier Element
	fire = "Fire elemental damage type"
	ice  = "Ice elemental damage type"

define Creature
	attack  = ActionAttack
	element = Element

Creature FireArcher
	attack  = ActionAttack.ranged_weapon
	element = Element.fire

Creature IceWarrior
	attack  = ActionAttack.melee_weapon
	element = Element.ice

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compiles cleanly, no error.
//   FireArcher.attack  == ActionAttack.ranged_weapon (logical ID)
//   FireArcher.element == Element.fire (logical ID)
//   IceWarrior.attack  == ActionAttack.melee_weapon (logical ID)
//   IceWarrior.element == Element.ice (logical ID)
//   Each field's value must come from its own correct domain, with no cross-domain
//   leakage between `attack` and `element` on either instance.
